\description{
Finds the taxa whose abundance differs between groups (\code{stat.by}) or
changes with a numeric variable (\code{regr}). Unlike \code{\link[=taxa_stats]{taxa_stats()}}, which
tests each taxon's abundance on its own, these methods account for the
compositional nature of sequencing data: a sample's counts are a
sample from the community, so an increase in one taxon lowers the
observed proportions of all the others. The method is LinDA (Zhou et
al. 2022), built into rbiom.
}
\details{
LinDA expects unrarefied counts: it models differences in sequencing
depth itself, and rarefying discards data.

\strong{Counts.} The counts are summed to \code{rank} with \code{\link[=taxa_matrix]{taxa_matrix()}} (using
\code{unc} and \code{lineage}) and every sample with a value for all of the fields
used is included. Samples with an \code{NA} in \code{stat.by}, \code{regr},
\code{covariates}, \code{split.by}, or \code{strata} are dropped (with a message).

\strong{LinDA} (Zhou et al. 2022) fits one linear model per taxon to the
centered log2-ratios of the counts. Compositionality shifts every
taxon's coefficient by the same unknown amount; assuming that most taxa
don't change, that shift is the mode of the coefficients across taxa,
and subtracting it gives the log2 fold changes. rbiom's implementation
follows the published algorithm and the defaults of
\code{MicrobiomeStat::linda()}:
\enumerate{
\item Taxa present in fewer than \code{prev.cut} of the samples are removed,
and then any samples without counts.
\item Each taxon's relative abundances are winsorized at its 97th
percentile, and converted back to (rounded) counts.
\item Zeros: when the sequencing depth (log of the sample sums) is
associated with the model's variables (any p <= 0.1), each zero is
imputed as the sample's depth divided by the largest depth among
the samples where the taxon is absent. Otherwise a pseudo-count of
0.5 is added to every count.
\item Centered log2-ratio transformation (each sample's log2 counts minus
their mean).
\item A linear model per taxon, \code{clr ~ stat.by + covariates} (or
\code{regr}), fitted with \code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}}, or with \code{lmerTest::lmer()} and a
random intercept per stratum when \code{strata} is given. Numeric
variables are standardized for fitting; \code{regr}'s fold changes are
reported per unit.
\item Each comparison is a coefficient (\code{regr}, or a group versus the
first group) or a difference of two coefficients (two other
groups). Its bias is its mode across taxa, estimated by the
mean-shift algorithm with a Gaussian kernel (the same estimator as
\code{modeest::mlv(method = "meanshift")}), and is subtracted.
\item The standard error comes from the coefficients' covariance, and the
p-value from a t-test with the model's residual degrees of freedom
(Satterthwaite's for mixed models).
}

For a numeric \code{regr} and for comparisons with the first group, this
gives the results of \code{MicrobiomeStat::linda()}. For a comparison
between two other groups, \code{MicrobiomeStat::linda.wald.test()} would
subtract the difference of the two coefficients' modes; rbiom takes the
mode of the difference instead, which assumes that most taxa don't
differ between those two groups (rather than between each of them and
the first group), and doesn't depend on which group comes first.

One deliberate difference from \code{MicrobiomeStat::linda()} (version 1.4):
when it reports that sequencing depth is associated with the model, it
still adds the pseudo-count, because it compares
\code{"Imputation"} with \code{"imputation"}; rbiom imputes, as the paper
describes.
}
